WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.778 --> 00:00:03.756
Don't slow down.

00:00:04.645 --> 00:00:05.859
Well, that's -- [laughs]

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:07.809
Obviously he's trying to play very fast,

00:00:07.809 --> 00:00:10.140
but when you want to play fast,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.780
the first thing you have to do is relax.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.430
Hi, my name is Antonio Sánchez.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.029
I'm a jazz drummer and composer.

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:19.650
I'm a four-time Grammy winner,

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.740
and I did the film score for "Birdman."

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.101
Today, we're going to be
looking at drum scenes

00:00:25.101 --> 00:00:28.335
from films and TV and
judging how real they are.

00:00:30.057 --> 00:00:32.610
Are there any drummers in the house?

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.690
I play percussion.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.550
Just see if you can do what I do, OK?

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:36.538
Just give it a try.

00:00:36.538 --> 00:00:39.360
[laughs] The main thing to notice here

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.260
is they only have a
snare drum, a floor tom,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.160
which is basically not
even half of the drum set,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.893
and then the crash. It's
a kind of small cymbal.

00:00:49.893 --> 00:00:52.680
There's no bass drum,
and there's no hi-hat.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.364
This is the "School of
Rock." You need a bass drum.

00:00:55.364 --> 00:00:58.320
It's, like, the heartbeat
of the whole thing.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.780
So those are really not at all

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.550
the elements that you would need

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.694
to do a good rock groove.

00:01:10.843 --> 00:01:11.751
OK!

00:01:11.751 --> 00:01:15.380
So far, the kid is doing a good job.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.400
Because he's doing what he told him to do.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.260
The main challenge for somebody

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.540
that's coming from classical percussion,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.310
to play drum set,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.580
it would be how to coordinate
your feet and your hands.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.006
Classical percussionists
don't use their feet too much.

00:01:30.006 --> 00:01:31.220
Good. OK. No, that's bad.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.586
That's like "George of the Jungle."

00:01:32.586 --> 00:01:35.100
Play it up here on the
cymbal, but really light!

00:01:36.042 --> 00:01:38.358
Aw, that's it. OK. Let's go!

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:43.140
Musically, I don't think
it was the right choice.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.850
The song he's playing is

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.530
Deep Purple's "Smoke on the Water."

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.414
What you need in that
song is a big back beat.

00:01:49.414 --> 00:01:53.370
[imitates drums]

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.560
And, again, he doesn't have
the bass drum and the hi-hat.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.739
So I would have him maybe try to imitate

00:01:57.739 --> 00:02:00.186
the bass drum on the floor tom, like,

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:02.278
[imitates drums] something like that.

00:02:02.278 --> 00:02:04.380
Having said that, I know Jack Black,

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.940
he's a good musician.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.699
And, you know, what he told
him to play, he did play.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.079
I give it a 9 or a 10.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.980
One thing that I noticed
right off the bat,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.130
he looks relaxed. And
he's hitting hard, too.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.990
He's hitting as a rock drummer.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.520
That's not easy to do.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.950
It's kind of easy to get injured

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.270
if you don't know what you're doing.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.362
He definitely put some work into it.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.310
Everything so far that I've seen

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.860
totally matches with what I've heard.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.140
For example, in this section,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.420
I can hear a double bass drum,

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.480
which, in this case,
you would have a pedal,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.818
it's called a double pedal,

00:02:46.818 --> 00:02:51.270
that has two beaters that are
hitting the same bass drum.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.000
And then you play it like
if you had two bass drums,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.860
but you're only playing on one.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.010
And to me, it sounds like he
would have a double pedal.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.843
So you can hear the
[imitates hitting bass drum]

00:03:00.843 --> 00:03:02.880
from the double bass drum

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.047
and the hands just doing the cymbal.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.651
It doesn't sound to me
like they're trying to

00:03:09.651 --> 00:03:12.519
focus on a groove or a specific tempo.

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:14.730
It's more like an energy.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.363
So she's screaming and
he's playing fast and loud,

00:03:18.363 --> 00:03:19.800
playing around a tonal center,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.620
and making sort of a wall of sound.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.270
Like, almost if they're playing free,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.340
and that seems like the right choice

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.480
for that particular tune.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.380
I would give it a 10.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.090
It impressed me how well he played

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.765
for him not to be a professional drummer.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:36.513
Don't slow down.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.950
I'm just not sure what
the drummer is doing.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.750
Obviously he's trying to play very fast.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.240
But when you want to play fast,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.880
the first thing you have to do is relax

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.200
instead of tensing up.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.720
That's why you have to
start practicing slow,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.030
so that you can feel the movement,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.100
how everything works together,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.420
the right hand with the left hand

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.980
and the left foot and the right foot,

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.982
and then you start speeding
up little by little.

00:04:01.982 --> 00:04:04.260
But just looking at the faces he's making

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.643
while he's playing, it was
like if he was lifting weights

00:04:06.643 --> 00:04:10.470
and trying to see how much
he can go before breaking.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:11.760
The body is one thing,

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.115
but if you start getting
tensed up in your head,

00:04:16.115 --> 00:04:18.780
then that's the beginning of the end.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:19.976
Pick it up!

00:04:21.835 --> 00:04:22.712
Faster!

00:04:22.712 --> 00:04:25.507
I didn't hear the song, but traditionally,

00:04:25.507 --> 00:04:28.387
"Caravan" is not a very hard song to play,

00:04:28.387 --> 00:04:30.570
and it's not a very fast
song to play either.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.911
For example, there are other songs

00:04:32.911 --> 00:04:36.030
that are traditionally very fast.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.623
There's one called "Lover,"

00:04:38.623 --> 00:04:43.006
and the melody goes [humming]

00:04:43.006 --> 00:04:46.789
[humming].

00:04:46.789 --> 00:04:48.262
The drums are supposed to play

00:04:48.262 --> 00:04:50.153
kind of double time on top of it.

00:04:50.153 --> 00:04:52.260
"Caravan" is not one of those.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.114
"Caravan" is usually a medium up,

00:04:55.114 --> 00:05:00.114
which is [humming].

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.590
Although I didn't hear the arrangement.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.530
Maybe if they had an amazing arrangement

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.071
that justifies the song being
that fast, you can do it.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.880
Whatever it is that he's
doing, it's not music.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.670
I don't hear any real tempo.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.740
I don't hear a clear pattern.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.260
When you're playing jazz
at a very fast tempo,

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.410
you start usually with quarter
notes on the ride cymbals,

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:29.610
which is 1, 2, 3, 4; 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.862
Then the right pattern,

00:05:31.862 --> 00:05:36.090
which is the quintessential
jazz bepop pattern,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.220
which is what makes it swing,

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.307
is 1, 2, 3, 4; 1, 2, 3, 4;

00:05:42.307 --> 00:05:44.490
ching, ching, cha-ching, ching, cha-ching.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:45.770
We don't have a hi-hat today,

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.600
but that would be going in two and four.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.260
1, 2, 3, 4; 1 2, 3, 4.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:53.307
That's the basic pulse.

00:05:53.307 --> 00:05:54.965
And then on your left hand,

00:05:54.965 --> 00:05:57.450
you would be just kind of conversing

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:59.640
on what's going on with the music.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.032
And then you speed that up.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.640
But nothing he was doing
was relating to that.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.580
There's no pulse, there's no
finesse, there's no nuance.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.080
It's just like, kshh,

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.556
like a broken washing machine.

00:06:15.556 --> 00:06:18.306
[cowbell ringing on tempo]

00:06:18.306 --> 00:06:21.150
That's the pulse. That's
what he should be playing to,

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.870
but you would hear both
of them intersecting.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.380
And to my ears, they're really not.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.263
If you want your drummer to play

00:06:30.263 --> 00:06:34.029
[imitates faster drumming],

00:06:34.029 --> 00:06:35.280
you would not play that.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.700
But the cowbell's a great instrument too,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.159
to play really loud so that
the drummer could hear it.

00:06:41.159 --> 00:06:42.668
Faster!

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.050
Well, that's -- [laughs].

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:50.401
The amount of blood that there
is on the drums right now,

00:06:50.401 --> 00:06:51.360
that's kind of staggering.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.040
The only times where I bled

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.820
is I hit the edge of the hi-hat.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.940
You can open one of your
knuckles a little bit.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.340
But that amount of blood on the drums,

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.490
that's a sign that something
very bad is being done.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.640
I would probably rate it at a 1.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.840
It kind of shows that speed
for the sake of speed,

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.270
there's no finesse, there's no nuance,

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:16.861
so there's no music, really.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.340
My shoulders and my neck hurt
just by watching him play.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.520
I remember this guy. There
was a famous clip going on.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:27.900
He was doing all that stuff.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.132
I remember watching
it, and I was laughing,

00:07:30.132 --> 00:07:33.150
because it looked so crazy
but he sounded really good.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.692
To me, it's very hard.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:38.610
Because I've always emphasized trying to

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.380
make the least amount of movement

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.750
and get the most out of the sound.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:46.350
And he's doing the most amount
of movement he possibly can.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.735
Having said that, he plays great.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:55.260
The grooves and licks
that Steve is playing,

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.550
they're fairly complicated.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.427
A lick is basically a
pattern that you learn

00:07:59.427 --> 00:08:02.430
that you can throw at any given moment.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.017
So if you do [imitates drumming],

00:08:04.017 --> 00:08:04.982
that's one lick.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:11.610
Well, Kevin didn't
necessarily play any drums.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.530
He just hit the cymbal a couple of times.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:14.850
And that's it.

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:17.166
You can tell he's never really

00:08:17.166 --> 00:08:18.991
played a drum set in his life,

00:08:18.991 --> 00:08:21.917
which is fine. I think
that's the idea of the scene.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.620
Twirling, throwing sticks up in the air.

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:29.550
They're not necessarily
connected to the music,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.180
but if you want cool
visuals and showmanship,

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.190
they can be very effective.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:38.190
When you do those kinds of tricks

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.050
and the music suffers because of that,

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:43.410
then I think you should
reevaluate what you're doing.

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:44.400
10, for sure.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.680
Everything they're trying to
demonstrate that he's playing,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:47.523
he's doing it.

00:08:51.055 --> 00:08:51.930
It wasn't that bad.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.240
That's a very simple drumbeat.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.370
When it comes to drumming styles,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:00.090
there are some that are
inherently easier than others.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.400
Rock is usually where you start

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:06.390
because it's one of the most simple ways

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:09.150
to create already, like, a cool beat.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:12.927
If you're going to do [imitates drumming],

00:09:12.927 --> 00:09:14.490
that already sounds good.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:18.870
Anybody with decent hand-eye coordination

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:22.003
could probably pull that
off in a day or two.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:30.725
It's not totally lining up
with what you're hearing.

00:09:30.725 --> 00:09:32.070
Movies, for some reason,

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.693
they usually don't get the syncing

00:09:34.693 --> 00:09:37.620
with the image and the
music really well done.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.240
Drums is very precise.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.770
With guitars or bass,

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.300
you cannot really tell that much

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.880
because there's not that clear attack,

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.953
as when you are trying to hit something.

00:09:48.467 --> 00:09:50.640
I mean, she's kind of doing it.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.400
The technique leaves a lot to be desired

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.220
because she's grabbing the
sticks so in the middle.

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:57.990
You get no power from that,

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:00.000
because all of the weight

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.370
that you would be able
to hit the drums with,

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:03.390
it's not there.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:06.360
You need to find the place in the stick

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.300
where it will bounce by itself the most.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.910
If you hold it really far back,

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.456
it would just go like
[imitates sound trailing off]

00:10:13.456 --> 00:10:14.878
and just die out.

00:10:14.878 --> 00:10:18.090
And if you're holding really
to the front of the stick,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.880
the stick would bounce and
then kind of fall back.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.070
So you have to find the sweet spot.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:25.518
And usually the sweet spot is, like,

00:10:25.518 --> 00:10:28.440
three-quarters back into the stick.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.440
In that situation, the drums
would sound really puny.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:35.880
You can totally look that relaxing

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:37.530
and play a good rock beat.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.410
Drummers, it doesn't matter
if they're jazz or rock,

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.240
they should be relaxed.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.450
Usually rock drummers have a
little bit more of an attitude,

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:48.570
a lot more kind of big gestures,

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:51.660
even if they're doing something
simple, like she's doing.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.070
Instead of playing on the hi-hat,

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:54.660
she's playing on the floor tom.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.370
So [imitates drums],

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:58.290
which is like The White Stripes.

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:00.780
That's what they would be known for,

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:02.340
that kind of a beat.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.620
I would say probably a 7 or an 8.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:06.404
She didn't do such a bad job.

00:11:11.097 --> 00:11:12.870
So, that's one thing.

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:16.596
Many people maybe don't know
exactly what it's called

00:11:16.596 --> 00:11:19.110
or why people play this way.

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:21.750
What he's playing here is
called traditional grip,

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:23.580
which takes a lot longer to learn.

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:25.920
When you play traditional grip,

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.071
these two fingers are
kind of not doing much.

00:11:29.071 --> 00:11:32.160
You have to find different ways

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.190
to make these two fingers

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:37.830
do the work of all these four fingers.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:39.720
And then you're doing
it with your weak hand,

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:40.740
which is the left,

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.830
in terms of like he's doing
it or the way I do it.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.930
So then you have to work twice as hard

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:49.050
to get some of the same finesse and speed.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.880
When you think, why don't both hands

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.350
just do exactly the same thing?

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:53.820
That's called match grip.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.390
The traditional grip comes
from the old marching bands.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.667
People would play this way so that

00:11:59.667 --> 00:12:02.520
there was an incline on the snare drum,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.687
and then it was very easy to
play right here by your side

00:12:05.687 --> 00:12:07.770
while you were walking or marching.

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:10.050
I think his playing
showcases the fact that

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:12.720
he's coming more from a jazz background.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.060
Usually, people that play traditional grip

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:16.677
come more from jazz.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:23.573
Sounds good.

00:12:23.573 --> 00:12:24.908
What he started playing,

00:12:24.908 --> 00:12:27.840
[imitates drumming],

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.990
was totally accurate for that time period,

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.390
in the '60s, and '70s too.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:36.455
Like, for example, The Beach
Boys had a lot of songs

00:12:36.455 --> 00:12:38.823
that had that kind of vibe.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.890
That would be very similar to the kit

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.620
that Ringo Starr would
play with The Beatles.

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:51.510
It's a four-piece kit, they call it,

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.040
so, yeah, it's accurate.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.531
He sounded good, and it
felt good. I think 10.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.670
That's 100% accurate.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:05.465
The first thing you would
do when you sit down

00:13:05.465 --> 00:13:07.770
would be just to hit
everything a couple of times

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.998
just to see what it sounds like.

00:13:09.998 --> 00:13:11.940
And then you would probably start

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:15.840
with some kind of groove
or couple of fills.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.110
Depends also what style
of drumming you like

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:20.130
and what you play.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:23.263
But if you were going to play
rock, that's pretty much it.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:29.190
That would have been my
dream drum kit back then.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.230
In the '80s, this was a thing.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:35.190
For example, Rush, Neil
Peart had a gigantic kit.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:39.778
This drum set is in what
they call a cage, which is,

00:13:39.778 --> 00:13:43.050
it's, like, a metal bar that
goes around the drum set

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:45.120
where you set everything on top,

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.580
which was very popular during the '80s.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.620
But what I notice is that

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.610
he's just using the left side of the kit.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:56.550
Normally, your right leg would
be on the right bass drum.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:14:00.300
And I know Dana Carvey, he
actually plays the drums,

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.850
and he's absolutely playing
everything we heard.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.340
Drum solos can be anything.

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:11.940
You can have a completely
prepared drum solo,

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.880
or you can completely improvise
what you're going to do.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:17.976
So there's really no correct

00:14:17.976 --> 00:14:20.520
or incorrect way to play a solo.

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:24.270
Usually, in a rock context,
that's fairly accurate,

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:27.802
but you kind of keep some
kind of pulse happening,

00:14:27.802 --> 00:14:29.820
and then you do a bunch of fills.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:32.160
And it's just kind of meant
to get the crowd going.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:33.960
What he did is not particularly hard,

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:36.810
but it's not particularly easy either.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:39.660
You need a couple of
years under your belt.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.270
But the drumming itself
is definitely real.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:43.860
10, for sure.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:47.580
Lately, my favorite drum movie,

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:50.400
even though the plot is very sad,

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:52.320
is the "Sound of Metal."

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.500
Everything you see
gives you the impression

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:56.880
that it's actually happening.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:57.930
Thanks for watching.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.580
And if you enjoyed this video,

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.863
why not click on the video above?

